WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:07.940
Hi everyone, it's Jerry. There's an instructional
queen trap out of the Fort Knox variation

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:13.720
of the French Defense that is capable of catching
even a Grandmaster opponent. I'm able to say

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:19.849
this because I experienced that first hand.
I had a Grandmaster opponent make an inaccuracy

00:00:19.849 --> 00:00:26.149
early on. I was aware of the trap, and I was
able to take advantage of it. Now I feel that

00:00:26.149 --> 00:00:32.800
it's instructive because from the variation
itself, there's an important strategical concept

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:39.600
to take away from it, and we also see highlighted
from the trap itself, the importance of move

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.589
order...especially in the opening. Why it's
a bad idea to bring the queen out early.

00:00:44.589 --> 00:00:50.800
The importance of calculating accurately,
and in many case deep enough. And also the

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.469
power of in-between moves, and discovered
attacks. So let's see how that all works.

00:00:56.469 --> 00:01:03.469
It starts off after e4, we have e6, the French
Defense. d4, d5, knight c3...this knight's

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:10.159
deployment is irrelevant...he can also play
to d2 since after d takes e the knight is

00:01:10.159 --> 00:01:17.159
recapturing. Bishop d7, the beginning of the
Fort Knox variation. And the idea behind it

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:24.380
is to do what exactly? The strategical concept
behind this variation is to give up the light-square

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:29.750
bishop, and then make sure you have pawns
around on light squares to compensate for

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:36.750
his loss. But we don't go down that road.
Instead we get to see an inaccuracy by black

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:46.869
right away. Knight f6, this is not a good
move. What's better is to play the queen knight

00:01:46.869 --> 00:01:52.789
here, and only then deploy the knight to f6.
Then black would be in a position to meet

00:01:52.789 --> 00:01:59.429
knight takes with the queen knight recapturing.
But it's very easy to mix up the move order.

00:01:59.429 --> 00:02:06.299
Knight f6, and now we capture the knight.
What would be best at this point is to recapture

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:10.649
with the pawn. But I believe most players
as black would be recapturing with the queen,

00:02:10.649 --> 00:02:17.649
keeping the pawn structure as is. But after
this recapture the black queen is simply lost.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:23.000
If you'd like to, pause the video. See if
you could figure out how white should be responding

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:30.000
from this position. Okay, here's the solution...it's
bishop g5. The queen is running very short

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:37.680
on squares. And I think many players as black
would be thinking bishop g5 is a bad move

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:46.940
because there is this potential in-between
shot, bishop takes knight. And now what do

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:51.750
you do as white? If you're recapturing with
the queen...well this isn't good. Material

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:57.040
is balanced and what do you have to show for
this smashed opened pawn structure? Not a

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:03.750
whole lot. Similarly, capturing the queen...this
is even worse. You're down a piece as white.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:08.780
If the white bishop moves away, so too does
the black bishop. If you capture the bishop,

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:14.370
black captures your bishop. Black is up a
knight in this variation. The trick here is

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:21.370
to meet this in-between move with an in-between
move yourself. Queen to d2. A very very easy

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:29.900
move to miss in your calculations if you're
playing on the black side. The queen is defending

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:36.580
the bishop, of course she's out of the line
of fire of the bishop on f3, and she is indirectly

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:43.580
controlling d4. If now queen takes d4, we
get to see now a discovered attack with bishop

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:49.840
b5. The king is in check, the queen is dead.
Now the best continuation for black at this

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:56.080
point would be to actually capture on g2,
allow the queen to be captured, the bishop

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:03.080
takes the rook. From this position, the material
is actually balanced, but black is still having

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:09.620
a difficult time because this pawn is under
fire...white also has a lead in development,

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:16.620
and this bishop runs the risk of maybe getting
captured. If something like c6, king to e2.

00:04:19.409 --> 00:04:25.479
If the bishop is going to d5, we can see c4.
He's running short on squares. White has all

00:04:25.479 --> 00:04:32.240
their pieces working. Now there's g7 coming
under fire. In short, this is not the road

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:38.569
to go down from this point right here. If
as black you are recapturing on f6, you are

00:04:38.569 --> 00:04:45.569
officially losing your queen after this bishop
to g5 move.

